{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3278","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3278","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3278\",\"parent_revision\":\"74bf63d375d30e06d2d86b2e41dc9f849cd720daee46e4f0c35a4935358883e8\",\"root_revision\":\"74bf63d375d30e06d2d86b2e41dc9f849cd720daee46e4f0c35a4935358883e8\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-28\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-29 while running `/dh:work-backlog-item work --auto #3268`: plan Pb31107bf's dependency graph correctly declares T1, T3, T4, T5 as mutually independent (`parallelize_with` each other, no dependency edges), so `implement-feature`'s Progress Loop dispatched all four concurrently as designed. Task T1's completion report stated: \\\"first `sam_task state` call hit a concurrent-write 'Content revision no longer matches' \u2014 expected since T3/T4/T5 run in parallel on the same plan; retry succeeded.\\\"\\n\\nRead `sam_schema/core/backends/content.py:64-121` (`ContentTaskProvider`) to find the mechanism. `_flush(plan_id, ...)` (line 85-94) serializes `self._plans[plan_id]` \u2014 the entire plan record, including every task in it \u2014 into one JSON blob and writes it via `provider.put_content(ContentWrite(reference=ContentRef(kind=ContentKind.PLAN, name=plan_id), ..., expected_revision=self._revisions.get(plan_id, \\\"\\\")))`. `self._revisions` (line 71) is a `dict[str, str]` keyed by `plan_id` only \u2014 one revision string per plan, not per task. `_mutate()` (line 96-116) wraps every task-level operation (status change, claim, field update) in this same read-whole-plan / write-whole-plan-with-single-revision-check pattern, catching `ContentProviderError` on mismatch, refreshing, and re-raising for the caller to retry.\\n\\n**Consequence**: any two tasks in the same plan that are logically independent (per the dependency graph) still contend for the same document-level revision the moment both attempt a task-state write inside the same time window. The observed retry succeeded and no data was lost \u2014 the optimistic-concurrency retry path itself is not broken \u2014 but the design guarantees a nonzero collision probability that scales with how many tasks in a wave write state concurrently, which directly contradicts a task graph's premise that independent nodes have zero chance of interfering with each other. Under higher parallelism (e.g. `TeamCreate` dispatching a full wave of 5+ ready tasks under `full_auto` autonomy) this will occur more often, and the retry burden falls on whichever caller code wraps `sam_task` \u2014 not verified here whether every call site actually retries on this error, only that `dh:task-worker`'s manual retry succeeded once in this session.\\n\\nUser-provided context: the plan is meant to be \\\"a graph of nodes\\\" with \\\"no chance of conflict at all ever\\\" between independent parallel tasks \u2014 this contradicts that design intent. Root cause not yet diagnosed beyond the mechanism traced above (whether the fix is per-task content records, per-task revision tracking within one plan document, or a different storage decomposition is a design question for grooming, not decided here).\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 development-harness /dh:work-backlog-item work --auto #3268, plan Pb31107bf\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"291998b0401673036a6f4295f6a560d692b4c0337cb8171a509dd5f25ecf5782\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRSCCRQ\"}"}